Specific energy, critical depth and hydraulic jumps (@fd-open-channel: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @fluid-dynamics, #source "../sources/Advanced-Hydraulics-Kartha.lingua#r_1MCV024RY5CA95WTHY708FP7VT", #source "../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA", #fluid-dynamics "../geophysical/Stable-stratification-buoyancy-frequency-and-internal-waves.lingua#r_691GN8PDCYCW2BN2T3ETWSNC4B") { r_5R5PG02RW7F1B7X6QWCR4MVZHN
**Read:** [Advanced Hydraulics — Suresh A. Kartha](../sources/Advanced-Hydraulics-Kartha.lingua#r_1MCV024RY5CA95WTHY708FP7VT), specific energy, critical flow, momentum, hydraulic jumps and varied flow; [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), conservation and shallow-water waves.

For a rectangular channel with discharge per width q, specific energy relative to the bed is E=h+q²/(2gh²) under a uniform-profile idealization. Its minimum occurs at critical depth h_c=(q²/g)^(1/3). Two depths can share the same specific energy above that minimum, but they lie on different flow branches.

Across a short ideal hydraulic jump on a horizontal bed, conserve discharge and streamwise momentum to derive h_2/h_1=[√(1+8Fr_1²)−1]/2 for the supercritical upstream branch. Mechanical energy decreases; it is not a conserved condition for the jump. For Fr_1=2 the depth ratio is approximately 2.3723.

Compare alternate depths at equal energy with conjugate depths connected by a jump. Extend to gradually varied flow using a stated friction relation and bed profile, identifying where its approximation fails. Actual channels add nonuniform sections, entrainment, sediment, roughness and unsteady boundary conditions; the ideal relations supply foundations for a more complete hydraulic study.

**Continue:** [Stable stratification, buoyancy frequency and internal waves](../geophysical/Stable-stratification-buoyancy-frequency-and-internal-waves.lingua#r_691GN8PDCYCW2BN2T3ETWSNC4B).

